= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <closed sets> contain the empty set and the whole space, are closed under arbitrary intersections, and are closed under finite unions. These conditions are equivalent, by complements, to the open-set axioms of a <topology>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

A map is <continuous> when the inverse image of every <open set> is open. Equivalently, the inverse image of every <closed set> is closed.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The definition of <convergence of a sequence in a topological space> to $\ell$ is that every open neighborhood $U$ of $\ell$ contains all sufficiently late terms: there exists $N$ such that $x_n\in U$ for every $n\geq N$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The empty set is countable, and the whole space contains $a$, so both belong to the proposed closed family. A finite union contains $a$ if any member does; otherwise it is a finite union of countable sets and remains countable. For an arbitrary intersection, if every member contains $a$, so does the intersection. Otherwise some member excludes $a$ and is countable, and the intersection is a subset of it, hence countable. Thus **all closed-set <topology> axioms hold**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $E=\{x:f(x)\ne f(a)\}$ be countable. For a closed subset $C$ of the real line, if $f(a)\in C$, its inverse image contains $a$ and is closed by definition. If $f(a)\notin C$, its inverse image lies inside $E$ and is countable, hence is again closed. Every closed inverse image is therefore closed, proving **$f$ is continuous**.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The set $S=\{x_n:x_n\ne\ell\}$ is countable, hence closed, and does not contain $\ell$. Its complement is an open neighborhood of $\ell$. Convergence therefore places every sufficiently late term outside $S$, which is possible only if those terms equal $\ell$. Thus **the <sequence> is eventually constant at its limit**, and the set of exceptional indices is finite. This conclusion concerns repeated indices as well as distinct values.

= 2
{parent=2009/E}
{scope}
{title2=E2}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A subset is <compact> if every family of open sets covering it has a finite subfamily still covering it. Equivalently, its subspace <topology> has the finite-subcover property.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $\{U_i\}$ be an open cover of $f(X)$. By <continuity> the inverse images $f^{-1}(U_i)$ are open and cover $X$. Compactness provides finitely many of them covering $X$, and the corresponding $U_i$ then cover $f(X)$. Thus **a continuous image of a <compact> space is <compact>**.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The family contains the whole space and the empty set. A finite intersection of nontrivial members is $(\max_j a_j,\infty)$. A nonempty union of rays is $(\inf_j a_j,\infty)$ when the infimum is finite, and is the whole line when the infimum is $-\infty$. Empty unions and unions involving the whole space are already included. Hence **$\tau$ is a <topology>**. This is the <upper-ray topology on the real line>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

If $A$ has a smallest element $a_0$, any open cover has a member containing $a_0$. This member is either the whole line or a ray $(c,\infty)$ with $c<a_0$, so it already contains all of $A$. Thus a single member suffices and $A$ is <compact>.

If $A$ has no smallest element, the family $\{(a,\infty):a\in A\}$ covers $A$: each point has another point of $A$ below it. A finite subfamily has union $(\min_j a_j,\infty)$, which misses its smallest listed endpoint, itself a point of $A$. No finite subcover exists. Therefore **a nonempty subset is <compact> exactly when it has a smallest element** in this <topology>.

= F
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Multivariable Calculus}

= 1
{parent=2009/F}
{scope}
{title2=F1}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The full-<rank> <constraint qualification> gives a multiplier vector $\lambda\in\mathbb R^2$ such that
$$
\boxed{\nabla g(x_0)=Df(x_0)^T\lambda.}
$$
Equivalently the <gradient> of the objective lies in the span of the two constraint gradients. The point also satisfies $f(x_0)=0$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The constraint intersection is nonempty, closed and bounded, hence <compact>, so extrema exist. The two constraint gradients are $(x/2,2y,2z)$ and $(1,1,-1)$. If the first were $t$ times the second, the plane condition would give $2t+t/2+t/2=0$, so $t=0$ and all coordinates would vanish, contradicting the ellipsoid equation. Their <rank> is therefore two at every feasible point, justifying the <Lagrange multiplier> equations.

These equations are
$$
(1,2,0)=\lambda(x/2,2y,2z)+\mu(1,1,-1),\quad z=x+y.
$$
Eliminating $\mu$ gives $1=\lambda(5x/2+2y)$ and $2=\lambda(2x+4y)$. The first equation makes $\lambda\ne0$; subtracting twice the first from the second forces $x=0$. Hence $z=y$, and the ellipsoid gives $y=z=\pm1/\sqrt2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{g_{\max}=\sqrt2\text{ at }(0,1/\sqrt2,1/\sqrt2),\qquad
 g_{\min}=-\sqrt2\text{ at }(0,-1/\sqrt2,-1/\sqrt2).}
$$
All candidates were found, and <compactness> ensures they include the extrema.

= G
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= 1
{parent=2009/G}
{scope}
{title2=G1}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

Take an admissible variation $u+\epsilon\eta$ with $\eta=0$ on the boundary. Differentiating the functional at an extremum gives
$$
0=\int_V(F_u\eta+F_{u_x}\eta_x+F_{u_y}\eta_y+F_{u_z}\eta_z)dV.
$$
Integration by parts removes the derivatives from $\eta$; the boundary term vanishes. The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> yields
$$
\boxed{\partial_xF_{u_x}+\partial_yF_{u_y}+\partial_zF_{u_z}-F_u=0.}
$$
For the specified squared-<gradient> energy this is $2\Delta u=0$, so $u$ is harmonic. The affine function $\ell=ax+by+cz$ is harmonic and satisfies the boundary data.

To verify a global minimum, write $u=\ell+v$ with zero boundary trace. Then
$$
I[u]=I[\ell]+2\int_V\nabla\ell\cdot\nabla v\,dV+\int_V|\nabla v|^2dV.
$$
The middle term vanishes by integration by parts, since $\Delta\ell=0$ and $v=0$ at the boundary. Hence $I[u]\geq I[\ell]$, and
$$
\boxed{\min I=(a^2+b^2+c^2)\operatorname{vol}V.}
$$
This proves minimality directly, rather than relying only on a necessary Euler-Lagrange equation.

= H
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= 1
{parent=2009/H}
{scope}
{title2=H1}

= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <Lagrangian> is $L=\sum_i m_i|\dot q_i|^2/2-f(r)$. Away from a collision, its equations of motion are
$$
m_1\ddot q_1=-\frac{f'(r)}r(q_1-q_2)=:F,\qquad
m_2\ddot q_2=-F,\qquad m_3\ddot q_3=0.
$$
Summing forces proves conservation of total <linear momentum>. The total <mechanical energy> $E=\sum_i m_i|\dot q_i|^2/2+f(r)$ is also conserved: its derivative is $F\cdot(\dot q_1-\dot q_2)+f'(r)\dot r=0$, because $\dot r=(q_1-q_2)\cdot(\dot q_1-\dot q_2)/r$.

For the first proposed <angular momentum>,
$$
\frac d{dt}(m_1q_1\times\dot q_1)=q_1\times F
=\frac{f'(r)}r q_1\times q_2,
$$
which need not vanish. Thus **particle one's <angular momentum> about the fixed origin is not generally conserved**. Its force is central relative to particle two, not necessarily relative to that origin.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The third particle is free, so
$$
\frac d{dt}(m_3q_3\times\dot q_3)=q_3\times m_3\ddot q_3=0.
$$
Thus **particle three's <angular momentum> is conserved**.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The derivative of the pair's total <angular momentum> is $q_1\times F-q_2\times F=(q_1-q_2)\times F=0$, since $F$ is parallel to the separation vector. Therefore **the combined <angular momentum> of particles one and two is conserved**, despite the possible exchange between them individually.

= J
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= 1
{parent=2009/J}
{scope}
{title2=J1}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

For an observation point in the wire's plane, both $d\mathbf s$ and $\mathbf r-\mathbf s$ lie in that plane. Their cross product is normal to it, and the <Biot-Savart law> integrates only such normal vectors. Thus the <field> is perpendicular to the plane at every point off the wire.

At the centre, all four sides contribute in the same normal direction by the right-hand rule. A single side is distance $a$ from the centre, so its contribution is
$$
\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\int_{-a}^a\frac{a\,ds}{(a^2+s^2)^{3/2}}
=\frac{\mu_0I\sqrt2}{4\pi a}.
$$
Multiplying by four gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B_{\rm centre}=\frac{\mu_0I\sqrt2}{\pi a}\,\mathbf n,}
$$
where $\mathbf n$ is the oriented normal fixed by the current.

= K
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics and Waves}

= 1
{parent=2009/K}
{scope}
{title2=K1}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

For incompressible planar flow define the <streamfunction> by $u=\psi_y$, $v=-\psi_x$. A <velocity potential> satisfies $u=\phi_x$, $v=\phi_y$ when the flow is irrotational; the differential condition is $v_x-u_y=0$. The <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow> is $w=\phi+i\psi$, with derivative $w'=u-iv$.

For $w=m\log(z-z_0)/(2\pi)$, its derivative is $m/[2\pi(z-z_0)]$. This gives outward radial velocity $m/(2\pi r)$, whose flux across a circle is $2\pi r\cdot m/(2\pi r)=m$. It is therefore the stated line source.

Superposition gives
$$
w(z)=Uz+\frac m{2\pi}[\log z-\log(z-1)],\qquad
w'(z)=U-\frac m{2\pi z(z-1)}.
$$
For positive $U,m$, the stagnation points are
$$
\boxed{z_\pm=\frac{1\pm\sqrt{1+2m/(\pi U)}}2.}
$$
They lie on the real axis, one upstream of the source and the other downstream of the sink. Streamlines are level sets of $\psi=Uy+(m/2\pi)[\arg z-\arg(z-1)]$. The dividing <streamline> passes through both stagnation points and forms a <Rankine oval> enclosing the source and sink; outside, streamlines tend to uniform horizontal flow.

\Image[2009-rankine-oval.png]
{title=A representative source-sink pair in a uniform stream, with two stagnation points and a dividing Rankine oval}

= 2
{parent=2009/K}
{scope}
{title2=K2}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

Writing $z=re^{i\theta}$ gives $\phi=\Gamma\theta/(2\pi)$ and $\psi=-\Gamma\log r/(2\pi)$. Constant <streamfunction> means constant radius, so streamlines are circles. The tangential velocity is $v_\theta=\Gamma/(2\pi r)$, and its anticlockwise <circulation> is $\int_0^{2\pi}v_\theta r\,d\theta=\Gamma$.

Use an opposite vortex at $z=-1$ as the image of the vortex at one:
$$
\boxed{w(z)=-\frac{i\Gamma}{2\pi}[\log(z-1)-\log(z+1)].}
$$
On $z=iy$ the two distances are equal, so $\psi=0$ and the wall is a <streamline>. Its velocity follows from $w'=-i\Gamma/[\pi(z^2-1)]$: at the wall $u=0$ and $v=-\Gamma/[\pi(1+y^2)]$.

The <Bernoulli equation> gives $p-p_\infty=-\rho\Gamma^2/[2\pi^2(1+y^2)^2]$. Relative to the ambient pressure force, the fluid's excess force on the wall is $-\int(p-p_\infty)dy$ in the positive $x$ direction. Using the supplied integral,
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F_{\rm vortex}=\frac{\rho\Gamma^2}{4\pi}\,\mathbf e_x\quad\text{per unit width}.}
$$
Thus the wall is attracted toward the fluid and vortex. Subtracting the uniform ambient pressure isolates the finite force due to the vortex.

= M
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probability}

= 1
{parent=2009/M}
{scope}
{title2=M1}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Pointwise $Z^2\geq a^2\mathbf1_{\{|Z|\geq a\}}$. Taking expectations and dividing by $a^2>0$ proves the <Markov inequality>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(|Z|\geq a)\leq\mathbb EZ^2/a^2.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

The <joint density> of $X,Y$ is $\lambda^2e^{-\lambda(x+y)}$ for positive $x,y$. Inverting the transformation gives $x=uv$, $y=u(1-v)$, with absolute <Jacobian determinant> $u$ and domain $u>0$, $0<v<1$. The <change of variables for probability densities> gives
$$
\boxed{f_{U,V}(u,v)=\lambda^2u e^{-\lambda u}\mathbf1_{\{u>0,\ 0<v<1\}},\qquad
f_U(u)=\lambda^2u e^{-\lambda u}\mathbf1_{\{u>0\}}.}
$$
The <joint density> factors into the gamma density of $U$ and the uniform density of $V$ on $(0,1)$, so these two transformed variables are independent.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Part (b) gives $V_i\sim U(0,1)$, so $\mathbb EV_i=1/2$ and $\operatorname{Var}(V_i)=1/12$. Independence yields $\mathbb E(S_n/n)=1/2$ and $\operatorname{Var}(S_n/n)=1/(12n)$. Apply part (a) to $Z=S_n/n-1/2$:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\left(\left|S_n/n-1/2\right|\geq a\right)\leq\frac1{12na^2}.}
$$
This is a direct <Chebyshev inequality> for the sample <mean>.

= 2
{parent=2009/M}
{scope}
{title2=M2}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For $n=0$, the identity <matrix> gives the assertion. Suppose it holds at $n$. Conditioning on the state at time $n$ and using the <Markov property> gives
$$
\mathbb P(X_{n+1}=j\mid X_0=i)
=\sum_k\mathbb P(X_n=k\mid X_0=i)P_{kj}
=\sum_k(P^n)_{ik}P_{kj}=(P^{n+1})_{ij}.
$$
The nonnegative countable sum is justified by the law of total probability. Induction proves **the $n$-step <transition matrix> is $P^n$**.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Let $p_n=\mathbb P(X_n=1\mid X_0=1)$. Conditioning on the previous state gives $p_{n+1}=(1-a)p_n+b(1-p_n)=b+(1-a-b)p_n$, with $p_0=1$. Its fixed point is $b/(a+b)$; subtracting it leaves a geometric recursion. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_n=\frac b{a+b}+\frac a{a+b}(1-a-b)^n.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

After the first step, a chain starting at one enters the closed class $\{2,3\}$ with probability $p$, starting at two, or enters $\{4,5\}$ with probability $1-p$ and can never reach three. Within the first class, the probability of going from its first state to its second in $n-1$ steps is the complement of the formula in part (b). Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_n=3\mid X_0=1)
=p\frac a{a+b}\left[1-(1-a-b)^{n-1}\right],\qquad n\geq1.}
$$
At $n=1$ this correctly gives zero.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The total eventual mass of the closed class $\{4,5\}$ is $\lambda_4+\lambda_5+(1-p)\lambda_1$. The other closed class cannot contribute to state five. Since $|1-a-b|<1$, the two-state chain within the last class converges to its <stationary distribution>, with state-five mass $a/(a+b)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb P(X_n=5)
=\frac a{a+b}\bigl[\lambda_4+\lambda_5+(1-p)\lambda_1\bigr].}
$$

= 3
{parent=2009/M}
{scope}
{title2=M3}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The iid <central limit theorem> states that if $Y_1,Y_2,\ldots$ are independent and identically distributed with finite <mean> $\mu$ and <variance> $0<\sigma^2<\infty$, then
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sum_{i=1}^nY_i-n\mu}{\sigma\sqrt n}\xrightarrow{\mathrm d}N(0,1).}
$$
Equivalently, its cumulative distribution functions converge at every real argument to the standard normal cumulative distribution function.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <joint density> of $X,Y$ is $(2\pi)^{-1}e^{-(x^2+y^2)/2}$. The polar-coordinate Jacobian is $r$, so
$$
\boxed{f_{R,\Theta}(r,\theta)=\frac r{2\pi}e^{-r^2/2},\quad r>0,\ 0\leq\theta<2\pi.}
$$
Integration over the angular coordinate gives $f_R(r)=r e^{-r^2/2}$ for $r\geq0$ and zero otherwise. Thus $R$ has the <Rayleigh distribution>, while $\Theta$ is uniform and independent of $R$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $w\geq0$, $\mathbb P(W\leq w)=\mathbb P(R\leq\sqrt w)=1-e^{-w/2}$. Differentiation gives
$$
\boxed{f_W(w)=\tfrac12e^{-w/2}\mathbf1_{\{w\geq0\}}.}
$$
This is an <exponential distribution> of rate $1/2$, with <mean> two and <variance> four.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The independent variables $W_i$ have <mean> two and <variance> four by part (b). Their standardization is
$$
\frac{\sum_iW_i-2n}{2\sqrt n}
=\frac1{2\sqrt n}\sum_iW_i-\sqrt n.
$$
The <central limit theorem> therefore proves the claimed convergence to the standard normal law, whose continuous cumulative distribution function is $(2\pi)^{-1/2}\int_{-\infty}^x e^{-u^2/2}du$ at every real $x$.

= O
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= 1
{parent=2009/O}
{scope}
{title2=O1}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

For the continuous distribution in the question, $F$ maps the support monotonically from zero to one. For $0<t<1$, strict monotonicity gives
$$
\mathbb P(F(X)\leq t)=\mathbb P(X\leq F^{-1}(t))=t.
$$
Thus **$F(X)$ is uniform on $(0,1)$**, the <probability integral transform>.

The <order statistics> are the sample values sorted increasingly, $X_{(1)}\leq\cdots\leq X_{(n)}$. Monotonicity of $F$ makes $F(X_{(i)})$ the $i$th <order statistic> of independent uniforms. Its density is $n!t^{i-1}(1-t)^{n-i}/[(i-1)!(n-i)!]$, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E F(X_{(i)})=\frac{i}{n+1},\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.}
$$
This follows either by beta integrals or by the equal expected lengths of the $n+1$ uniform spacings.

For the stated log-logistic cumulative distribution,
$$
\log\frac{F(x)}{1-F(x)}=(\beta-1)\log x-(\beta-1)\log\alpha.
$$
Therefore plot $\log[p_i/(1-p_i)]$ vertically against $\log X_{(i)}$ horizontally, with plotting positions $p_i=i/(n+1)$. A plausible model gives an approximately straight line, with **slope $\beta-1$ and intercept $-(\beta-1)\log\alpha$**. This specifies the axes explicitly, since reversing them would invert the slope.

= 2
{parent=2009/O}
{scope}
{title2=O2}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

With immediate replacement and uniform mixing, each capture is a Bernoulli trial with tagged probability $p=k/N$. Counting failures before the $r$th tagged capture gives the stated <negative binomial distribution>. Independent runs on the successive days give the sample model.

Writing $S=\sum_i x_i$, the log-<likelihood> is $\ell(p)=nr\log p+S\log(1-p)+\text{constant}$. Its derivative vanishes at
$$
\boxed{\widehat p=\frac{nr}{nr+S}=\frac r{r+\overline x}.}
$$
The second derivative is negative. If $S=0$, this formula gives the boundary maximum $p=1$; on the strict physical parameter space $p<1$, it is a supremum rather than an attained interior maximum.

Using the supplied negative-binomial <mean>, $\mathbb ES=nr(1-p)/p$. Thus total <Fisher information> for the sample is
$$
\boxed{I_n(p)=-\mathbb E\ell''(p)=\frac{nr}{p^2}+\frac{nr}{p(1-p)}=\frac{nr}{p^2(1-p)}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For an interior true parameter and large $n$, standard <asymptotic normality of a maximum likelihood estimator> gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat p\ \dot\sim\ N\left(p,\frac{p^2(1-p)}{nr}\right).}
$$
Estimate its standard error by $s_e=\widehat p\sqrt{(1-\widehat p)/(nr)}$. A two-sided approximate <Wald confidence interval> is $(L,U)=(\widehat p-z_{1-\alpha/2}s_e,\widehat p+z_{1-\alpha/2}s_e)$, intersected with the parameter range $(0,1)$. The normal quantile is chosen so a standard normal lies between its positive and negative values with probability $1-\alpha$.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The relation $N=k/p$ is strictly decreasing. Thus an interval $0<L<U<1$ transforms by reversing endpoints:
$$
\boxed{N\in(k/U,k/L).}
$$
Keep integer population sizes satisfying $N>k$. If the clipped lower confidence endpoint for $p$ is zero, the transformed upper endpoint for $N$ is infinite. This monotone transformation preserves the approximate coverage probability supplied by the interval for $p$.

= 3
{parent=2009/O}
{scope}
{title2=O3}

= Solution
{parent=3}
{c}

An independent sum of normal variables is normal, with the means and variances added. Therefore $\overline X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2/n)$ and **$Z\sim N(0,1)$** exactly.

Under the null, $Z_0=\sqrt n(\overline X-\mu_0)/\sigma$ is standard normal. Reject for $Z_0>w$, where $w=\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha)$, giving size $\alpha$. For $\alpha=0.05$, the supplied table gives **the critical region $\overline X>\mu_0+1.64\sigma/\sqrt n$**, using its rounded quantile.

The <power function> is the probability of rejection when the true parameter is $\mu$. Recenter by that true <mean>:
$$
Q(\mu)=\mathbb P_\mu\left(\frac{\sqrt n(\overline X-\mu)}\sigma>w-\frac{\sqrt n(\mu-\mu_0)}\sigma\right)
=1-\Phi\left(w-\frac{\sqrt n(\mu-\mu_0)}\sigma\right).
$$
For $n=100$, $\sigma=2$, power at least $0.70$ means $w-5(\mu-\mu_0)\leq-0.52$. Hence, with the supplied rounding,
$$
\boxed{\mu\geq\mu_0+0.432.}
$$
The exact unrounded threshold is $\mu_0+(\Phi^{-1}(0.95)-\Phi^{-1}(0.30))/5$.

= P
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= 1
{parent=2009/P}
{scope}
{title2=P1}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

<Gershgorin circle theorem> places every complex <eigenvalue> of $A$ in at least one disk $D_i=\{z:|z-a_{ii}|\leq\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\}$. To prove it, take a nonzero <eigenvector> $v$ and choose $i$ with $|v_i|=\max_j|v_j|>0$. The $i$th <eigenvector> equation gives
$$
|\lambda-a_{ii}|\,|v_i|=\left|\sum_{j\ne i}a_{ij}v_j\right|
\leq\sum_{j\ne i}|a_{ij}|\,|v_i|.
$$
Division by $|v_i|$ proves the disk bound.

Here $C=I$, and the three disks for $B+\mu I$ have real centres $2+\mu$, $3+\mu$, $4+\mu$ and radii $2,3,4$ respectively. Every point in every disk has real part at least $\mu>0$. Hence zero cannot be an <eigenvalue>, proving **$B+\mu C$ is nonsingular for every $\mu>0$**.

= 2
{parent=2009/P}
{scope}
{title2=P2}

= Solution
{parent=2}
{c}

For any $q\in\Pi_n$, the difference $p-q$ lies in $\Pi_n$ and is orthogonal to $f-p$. The <Pythagorean theorem for inner product spaces> gives
$$
\|f-q\|^2=\|(f-p)+(p-q)\|^2=\|f-p\|^2+\|p-q\|^2\geq\|f-p\|^2.
$$
Thus the <orthogonality> condition proves minimality, and equality forces $q=p$.

Write the requested linear <polynomial> as $p=a+bx$. Orthogonality to one and $x$ gives
$$
\int_{-1}^1x^2(x^2-a-bx)dx=0,\qquad
\int_{-1}^1x^3(x^2-a-bx)dx=0.
$$
Odd integrals vanish, leaving $2/5-2a/3=0$ and $-2b/5=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{p(x)=3/5.}
$$
The weight's single zero does not destroy positive definiteness on continuous functions: a nonzero continuous function is nonzero on an interval containing points where $x^2>0$. The preceding projection argument therefore applies to this <inner product>.

