{title2=R1}

= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Label the outer square $000,001,011,010$ in cyclic order and the inner square $100,101,111,110$ in corresponding order. Join corresponding vertices radially. Each edge changes exactly one bit, so this gives every edge of $Q_3$, with no crossings.

\Image[2009-cube.png]
{title=A labelled planar cube with edge colours determined by the coordinate that changes}

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Partition the vertices by the parity of the number of one-bits. Every edge reverses that parity, giving a two-colouring; at least two colours are needed because an edge exists. Therefore **the vertex <chromatic number> is two**.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Every vertex has degree three, so its incident edges require three distinct colours. Three suffice by colouring an edge according to which of the three coordinates changes; every vertex has exactly one edge of each coordinate type. Thus **the <edge chromatic number> is three**.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The three faces incident to any cube vertex are pairwise adjacent along edges, forcing three colours. Three suffice by giving each pair of opposite faces a common colour, using a different colour for each of the three pairs. Hence **the face <chromatic number> is three**.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

A vertex is a bit string of length $k$, and adjacent vertices differ in one coordinate. Therefore the parity of the number of one-bits changes along every edge. The even-parity and odd-parity strings are a <bipartition>, proving **$Q_k$ is bipartite** for every $k\geq1$, in particular for the requested range.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

The hypercube is connected: successive coordinate flips reach any target bit string. Every vertex has degree $k$. A finite connected graph has an <Euler circuit> exactly when all vertex degrees are even. Therefore
$$
\boxed{Q_k\text{ is Eulerian exactly when }k\text{ is even}.}
$$

= v
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

If the printed $K_{33}$ means an ordinary $K_{3,3}$ subgraph, the claim is false. Any two distinct hypercube vertices have exactly two common neighbors when their Hamming distance is two, and no common neighbors otherwise. Three common neighbors, required by any pair of vertices on one side of $K_{3,3}$, are therefore impossible.

The valid intended statement is a <graph subdivision> of $K_{3,3}$. An explicit one in $Q_4$ has branch sets $A=\{0000,0011,1100\}$ and $B=\{0001,0010,0100\}$. Six of the nine branch-to-branch connections are direct edges: $0000$ joins all three vertices of $B$, $0011$ joins $0001,0010$, and $1100$ joins $0100$. The other three paths are
$$
\begin{aligned}
0011&\to0111\to0110\to0100,\\
1100&\to1101\to0101\to0001,\\
1100&\to1110\to1010\to0010.
\end{aligned}
$$
Every step changes one bit and the six internal vertices are distinct and are not branch vertices. Thus these paths and direct edges form the required topological subgraph. For $k>4$, append $k-4$ zeros to every label, embedding the construction in $Q_k$.

= vi
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}
{c}

The square $Q_2$ and the drawn cube $Q_3$ are planar. For $k\geq4$, the subdivision in part (v) prevents planarity by <Kuratowski's theorem>. One can also check the edge count directly: a triangle-free simple <planar graph> with $n\geq3$ vertices has at most $2n-4$ edges, whereas $Q_k$ has $n=2^k$ and $m=k2^{k-1}\geq2n$. Thus
$$
\boxed{Q_k\text{ is planar exactly for }k=2,3\text{ in the requested range }k\geq2.}
$$

= S
{parent=2009}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Simulation}

= 1
{parent=2009/S}
{scope}
{title2=S1}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For $0<r<1$, start at any $x\in\{0,\ldots,n\}$. Propose $Y$ uniformly from this same finite set and accept it with probability
$$
\boxed{\min\left\{1,\frac{\binom nY}{\binom nx}\left(\frac r{1-r}\right)^{Y-x}\right\}.}
$$
Otherwise retain $x$. The uniform proposal is symmetric, so this is the <Metropolis–Hastings algorithm> with target $\pi(\cdot\mid r)$. Detailed balance follows because accepted probability flow is proportional to $\min\{\pi(x),\pi(Y)\}$. The chain is irreducible and has positive self-transition probability, hence converges to that binomial equilibrium law. At the degenerate parameters zero or one, the target is simply a point mass and can be simulated as a constant chain.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Use the prior itself as an <independence> proposal: draw $J\geq1$ with $\mathbb P(J=j)=2^{-j}$ and propose $s=2^{-J}$. This indeed samples $p(s)=s$, since the geometric series sums to one. Starting from any allowed current value $r$, accept the proposal with probability
$$
\boxed{\min\left\{1,\left(\frac sr\right)^x
\left(\frac{1-s}{1-r}\right)^{n-x}\right\}.}
$$
The Hastings proposal ratio cancels the prior ratio in the posterior, leaving exactly the binomial <likelihood> ratio; its binomial coefficient also cancels. All allowed states communicate, and proposing the current state gives a self-loop. Detailed balance therefore gives the specified posterior as the stationary law, and the chain converges to it. Its normalizing constant is finite and positive because it is a prior average of a nonnegative, nonzero <likelihood>.

= T
